  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:Q1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Question",
    "Question"
   ],
   "title": "How does being responsible for a risky choice that affects a partner change how the decision-maker feels?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Does it feel different to have caused a bad outcome for someone than to have watched them cause it themselves? — Anchor: “The neural mechanisms underlying guilt evoked during such situations of social responsibility are still unknown.”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:Q2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Question",
    "Question"
   ],
   "title": "Which brain regions carry guilt and responsibility for a partner's outcomes, and how do they interact with prefrontal cortex?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Which parts of the brain respond to guilt and to being responsible for a partner, and how do they communicate with the prefrontal cortex? — Anchor: “Next we sought to uncover the neural mechanisms associated with our guilt effect”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:S1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Study",
    "Study"
   ],
   "title": "Study 1: behavioural experiment, 40 participants, three sessions outside the scanner",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Participants chose between safe and risky options for themselves (Solo), for themselves and a partner (Social), or watched the partner choose (Partner), rating happiness every two trials. The partner was another participant. — Anchor: “40 healthy participants (14 male, mean age 26.1, range 22 to 31) participated in Study 1 (behaviour only study)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:S2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Study",
    "Study"
   ],
   "title": "Study 2: fMRI experiment, 44 participants, two sessions in the scanner",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The same task inside a 3T scanner, with longer and variable intervals between events; the partner was an experimenter. All brain results come from this study. — Anchor: “44 healthy participants (19 male, mean (SD) age = 30.6 (6.5), range 23 to 50) participated in Study 2 (fMRI study)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Lottery choice task with Solo, Social and Partner conditions",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "On each trial a safe amount against a lottery with two equiprobable outcomes. Solo: the choice affects only the participant. Social: the participant chooses for both. Partner: the partner chooses for both. In Social and Partner, each player's lottery is drawn independently. — Anchor: “participants were presented with pairs of monetary options (a safe and a risky option; the risky option was a lottery with equally probable high and low outcomes)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Partner choices simulated by an expected-value-maximising algorithm",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "To keep the partner's behaviour identical across participants, the partner's choices were made by an algorithm that always picked the higher expected value. Participants were not told. — Anchor: “We contrasted this condition with similar choices made by a simple expected-value-maximizing algorithm posing for the partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Icebreaker game to create a social bond before the task",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Participant and partner met for about fifteen minutes and played a cooperative game, so that the partner's outcomes would matter emotionally. — Anchor: “participants were paired with a partner and played an “ice-breaker” game that created a positive social bond between them”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Momentary happiness probe every two trials",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "After every second outcome, participants rated how happy they felt on a continuous scale; the ratings are the dependent measure for the behavioural analyses. — Anchor: “Momentary happiness was assessed every two trials in a similar manner to previous studies by Rutledge and colleagues”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P5",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Risk-attitude estimation: risk premium and CARA expected utility",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Two measures of how risk-averse each participant was in each condition: a risk premium from a fitted logistic regression, and a constant-absolute-risk-aversion parameter ρ from expected utility theory. — Anchor: “we calculated for each participant a “risk premium”, defined as the difference between the expected value of a lottery and its certainty equivalent”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P6",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Five computational models of momentary happiness",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Basic, Inequality, Guilt-envy, Responsibility and Responsibility Redux: exponentially decaying influences of certain rewards, expected values and reward prediction errors, with the Responsibility models adding the partner's prediction errors split by who chose. Compared by AIC, BIC, R² and likelihood-ratio tests; parameter recovery on synthetic data. — Anchor: “we fitted computational models to each participant’s happiness data”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P7",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Linear mixed models of happiness after lottery outcomes",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Happiness after Social and Partner lottery outcomes modelled with the participant's outcome, the partner's outcome, who chose, and their interactions; the guilt effect is the partner-outcome × decision-maker interaction. — Anchor: “we analysed happiness values reported after outcomes of lottery choices in the Social and Partner conditions using conventional linear mixed models”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P8",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "fMRI acquisition, GLMs and region-of-interest mixed models",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "3T EPI BOLD; a conventional GLM of the trial events (GLM1) and a model-based GLM with the Responsibility model's variables as regressors (GLM2); regions of interest from the lottery-versus-safe outcome contrast analysed with linear mixed models. — Anchor: “We analysed the BOLD responses of brain regions engaged during decision-making and at the time of receiving the outcomes of the choice using conventional as well as computational model-based analyses”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P9",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Seed-to-voxel psychophysiological interaction from left insula and left STS",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Whole-brain connectivity during the choice phase from two seeds, looking for a Condition × Choice interaction. — Anchor: “we used the left insula and left STS as seed regions for whole-brain seed-to-voxel psychophysiological interaction analyses”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:P10",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Protocol",
    "Protocol"
   ],
   "title": "Comparison with the published guilt-related brain signature",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Each participant's guilt-related activation map was projected onto Yu et al.'s multivariate guilt signature; a positive dot product means resemblance. — Anchor: “we compared this brain signature to the neural responses obtained in our task”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/SourceDocument",
    "SourceDocument"
   ],
   "title": "Gädeke et al. (2026), Contributions of insula and superior temporal sulcus to interpersonal guilt and responsibility in social decisions, eLife 105391 v2",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The paper this graph is extracted from. — Anchor: “Contributions of insula and superior temporal sulcus to interpersonal guilt and responsibility in social decisions”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms11825",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/SourceDocument",
    "SourceDocument"
   ],
   "title": "Rutledge RB, de Berker AO, Espenhahn S, Dayan P, Dolan RJ (2016). The social contingency of momentary subjective well-being. Nature Communications 7:11825.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The paper whose happiness-modelling method this study adopts and extends to a social setting. — Anchor: “Following Rutledge and colleagues’ methodology, which considers that changes in momentary happiness in response to outcomes of a probabilistic reward task are explained by the combined influence of recent reward expectations and prediction errors”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Cited",
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Changes in momentary happiness during a probabilistic reward task are explained by recent reward expectations and the prediction errors arising from them.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Earlier work had shown that happiness in a gambling task follows recent rewards and surprises. — Anchor: “Following Rutledge and colleagues’ methodology, which considers that changes in momentary happiness in response to outcomes of a probabilistic reward task are explained by the combined influence of recent reward expectations and prediction errors”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms11825"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat0036",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/SourceDocument",
    "SourceDocument"
   ],
   "title": "Edelson MG, Polania R, Ruff CC, Fehr E, Hare TA (2018). Computational and neurobiological foundations of leadership decisions. Science 361:eaat0036.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The leadership study this paper compares its agency effect and its connectivity results with. — Anchor: “most participants displayed responsibility aversion, and this effect could not be explained by guilt, suggesting people associate a psychological cost with assuming responsibility for others’ outcomes”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Cited",
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Most participants show responsibility aversion — a psychological cost of deciding for others — that guilt does not explain.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Earlier work had found that people dislike being responsible for others' outcomes, independently of guilt. — Anchor: “most participants displayed responsibility aversion, and this effect could not be explained by guilt, suggesting people associate a psychological cost with assuming responsibility for others’ outcomes”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat0036"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/scan/nst090",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/SourceDocument",
    "SourceDocument"
   ],
   "title": "Yu H, Hu J, Hu L, Zhou X (2014). The voice of conscience: neural bases of interpersonal guilt and compensation. Social Cognitive and Affective Neuroscience 9:1150–1158.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The guilt paradigm that motivates the insula hypothesis. — Anchor: “The level of pain taken, indicative of guilt, and activations in anterior middle cingulate cortex and aIns were higher when the pain followed errors made only by the participant rather than by both players”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Cited",
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Anterior insula and anterior middle cingulate activity were higher when a partner's pain followed the participant's own errors.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Earlier work had found the anterior insula more active when a partner's pain followed the participant's own mistakes. — Anchor: “The level of pain taken, indicative of guilt, and activations in anterior middle cingulate cortex and aIns were higher when the pain followed errors made only by the participant rather than by both players”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1093/scan/nst090"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://doi.org/10.1093/cercor/bhz326",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/SourceDocument",
    "SourceDocument"
   ],
   "title": "Yu H, Koban L, Chang LJ, Wagner U, Krishnan A, Vuilleumier P, Zhou X, Wager TD (2020). A generalizable multivariate brain pattern for interpersonal guilt. Cerebral Cortex 30:3558–3572.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The source of the guilt-related brain signature this paper uses as an external yardstick. — Anchor: “A multivariate reanalysis of these two datasets revealed a neural signature for guilt (Yu et al., 2020)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Cited",
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "A multivariate reanalysis of two guilt studies yields a distributed guilt-related brain signature spanning anterior midcingulate, insula, IFG, inferior temporal cortex, thalamus and cerebellum.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Earlier work had derived a brain-wide signature of guilt from two studies. — Anchor: “A multivariate reanalysis of these two datasets revealed a neural signature for guilt (Yu et al., 2020)”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1093/cercor/bhz326"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:H1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Hypothesis",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Responsibility for a social choice that yields a low outcome for a partner produces interpersonal guilt, felt as a larger decrease in momentary happiness than when the partner made the same choice.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Being the one who chose, when the choice turns out badly for a partner, should hurt more than the same bad luck under the partner's own choice. — Anchor: “choosing a risky option in such a case may evoke guilt when the outcome for others is negative”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:H2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Hypothesis",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The anterior insula is the neural substrate of that guilt, responding more when the participant is responsible for a low outcome affecting the partner.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The anterior insula, already linked to guilt, should respond more when the participant is responsible for the partner's bad outcome. — Anchor: “Given the documented association between anterior insula and guilt (see Introduction)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:H3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Hypothesis",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Connectivity between the guilt- and responsibility-related regions and prefrontal cortex changes with who decides and with the type of choice.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "How the insula and STS couple with prefrontal cortex during a choice should depend on whether the choice is risky and whether it affects the partner. — Anchor: “We hypothesized that connectivity with regions that showed guilt- and responsibility-related responses during the outcome phase (see previous paragraph) might change depending on whether participants made decisions for themselves only or for themselves and their partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:H4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Hypothesis",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "A neural substrate tracks the participant's responsibility for the partner's outcomes, representing the partner's reward prediction errors more strongly when they arise from the participant's own choice.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Some brain region should track the partner's surprises more strongly when the participant, not the partner, caused them. — Anchor: “search for networks sensitive to reward prediction errors resulting from participant or partner choices”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Responsibility for a partner's bad lottery outcomes decreases participant happiness more than the same outcomes following partner choices, consistent with interpersonal guilt.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The same bad outcome for the partner lowered happiness more when the participant had made the choice, in both studies. — Anchor: “When the partner received the low lottery outcome, participant happiness was lower when they rather than the partner had chosen the lottery”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Computational models incorporating partner reward prediction errors differentiated by decision-maker (participant vs partner) best explain momentary happiness variations.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Among five models of what drives happiness, the one that separates out the partner's surprises caused by the participant's own choice fitted best, and that term mattered. — Anchor: “the Responsibility model fitted better than all the other models, including the Responsibility Redux model”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The participant's own reward prediction errors influenced happiness more than the partner's.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "A participant's own surprises affected their happiness more than the partner's did. — Anchor: “Participants’ reward prediction errors (sRPE) influenced happiness more than partner’s reward prediction errors”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Momentary happiness tracked the rewards both the participant and the partner received on the current trial.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Happiness rose and fell with the rewards both players had just received. — Anchor: “participant momentary happiness correlated with rewards obtained in the current trial by the participant and by the partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F5",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Being the decision-maker lowered happiness, independent of the outcome.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Simply being the one who decided made participants slightly less happy, whatever the outcome. — Anchor: “found happiness to be lower when the participant chose, independent of the outcome”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F6",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Responsibility did not change happiness after good outcomes for the partner.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "When the partner's outcome was good, it made no difference who had chosen. — Anchor: “Responsibility for choices did not influence happiness following positive lottery outcomes for the partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F7",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Participants show similar risk preferences when deciding for themselves versus for themselves and a partner, with a tendency toward higher risk aversion in the Social condition only in Study 1.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Participants were about as willing to take risks for themselves and a partner as for themselves alone. — Anchor: “participants showed very similar risk preferences when making decisions affecting only themselves (Solo condition) or themselves and their partner (Social condition)”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F8",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The guilt effect is associated with increased BOLD signal in the left anterior insula.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Insula activity rose more for the partner's bad outcomes when the participant had made the choice, mirroring the behavioural guilt effect. — Anchor: “activation in our insula ROIs increased in situations during which participants experienced guilt for low outcomes impacting their partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F9",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Functional connectivity between the left anterior insula and the right inferior frontal gyrus varies with choice and condition: highest for risky choices for oneself and safe choices for both players.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "How strongly the insula and the right inferior frontal gyrus were coupled depended on what was chosen and for whom. — Anchor: “a cluster in the right inferior frontal gyrus (IFG) whose connectivity to the insula (the seed region) was highest when participants made Risky choices for themselves and Safe choices for both players”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F10",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The right inferior frontal gyrus is sensitive to guilt-related information during social choices.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The authors suggest this prefrontal region handles guilt-related information while a social choice is being made. — Anchor: “suggesting that this part of prefrontal cortex is sensitive to guilt-related information during social choices”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F11",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Connectivity between the left STS and left inferior frontal gyrus showed the opposite pattern, but did not survive correction.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "A weaker, uncorrected result suggested the STS and the left inferior frontal gyrus couple in the opposite pattern. — Anchor: “connectivity with the left STS (the seed region) showed the opposite pattern”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F12",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The left superior temporal sulcus tracks partner reward prediction errors specifically when they result from the participant's choices.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "A region of the left superior temporal sulcus tracked the partner's surprises more when the participant had caused them. — Anchor: “We found this effect in one cluster within the left STS”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F13",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Choosing for both oneself and the partner, rather than for oneself alone, engaged the precuneus, left temporo-parietal junction and medial prefrontal cortex.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Choosing for two people engaged regions associated with thinking about others: precuneus, temporo-parietal junction and medial prefrontal cortex. — Anchor: “Three significant clusters of voxels were identified (Figure 4B and Supplementary Table 3), in the precuneus”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F14",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Receiving lottery outcomes, compared with safe outcomes, engaged bilateral anterior insula, dorsomedial prefrontal cortex, right STS, ventral striatum, right dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and inferior parietal lobe.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Receiving a lottery outcome engaged a set of regions associated with risk, including the anterior insula and ventral striatum. — Anchor: “Cluster of voxels more active during receipt of lottery outcomes than outcomes of safe choices were identified in the bilateral anterior insula”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:F15",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The guilt-related activation patterns resembled the published guilt-related brain signature.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Brain activity during the guilt effect resembled a published brain signature of guilt from other studies. — Anchor: “The “neurometrics” analysis of our guilt-related activation maps showed a significant similarity to a published guilt-related brain signature”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "The larger drop in happiness after the partner's low outcome is disappointment at the participant's own outcome, not guilt about the partner's.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps participants were just disappointed about their own outcome, not the partner's. — Anchor: “The “guilt effect” occurred whether the participant received the high lottery outcome”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Being responsible simply amplifies the participant's reaction to all of the partner's outcomes, good or bad.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps being responsible simply makes every outcome for the partner matter more, good or bad. — Anchor: “Responsibility for choices did not influence happiness following positive lottery outcomes for the partner”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Choosing for two people changes how much risk participants take, so the differences between conditions reflect different choices rather than guilt.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps people take different risks when choosing for two, and the differences between conditions follow from that rather than from guilt. — Anchor: “To better assess whether people’s risk preferences varied between the Solo and the Social condition”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Happiness tracks the inequality between the participant's and the partner's rewards; responsibility for the partner's surprises adds nothing.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps happiness just tracks the gap between what the two players got, with no role for who was responsible. — Anchor: “we assessed the fit of an ‘Inequality’ model that included one term modelling the difference between the participant and partner outcomes”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A5",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "What participants felt was empathy for the partner, a sense of failure or bad luck, or some emotion other than guilt.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps what participants felt was empathy, or a sense of failure or bad luck, rather than guilt. — Anchor: “we cannot exclude the possibility that they have instead or in addition felt empathy for their partner, a feeling of failure or bad luck, or some other emotion”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:A6",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Alternative",
    "cg:type/Claim",
    "Claim"
   ],
   "title": "Because the partner's choices were really an algorithm, participants were emotionally detached from the partner's outcomes and the effects do not reflect a social interaction.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Perhaps, because the partner was really an algorithm, participants were not emotionally engaged with the partner's outcomes at all. — Anchor: “The first limitation is that the partner’s decisions were in fact taken by an algorithm”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:K1",
