  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:K8",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Check",
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Ventral striatum activation increased with expected certain rewards and the expected values of chosen lotteries under the model-based regressor (left pFWE = 0.002, T = 5.63; right pFWE = 0.005, T = 5.46).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The ventral striatum tracked the model's expected rewards, confirming the model-based analysis works. — Anchor: “activation in bilateral ventral striatum indeed increased with the amount of expected certain rewards and the expected values of chosen lotteries”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "observationBase": "https://iiif.elifesciences.org/epp/105391%2Fv2%2Fcontent%2F107300v6_fig4.tif/full/max,/0/default.jpg"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E33",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "One left STS cluster responded more to the partner's reward prediction errors from participant than partner choices (pFWE = 0.022, T = 4.70, d = 0.53, 100 voxels, peak MNI [-52 -32 0]).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "A cluster in the left superior temporal sulcus responded more to the partner's surprises when the participant had caused them. — Anchor: “We found this effect in one cluster within the left STS (pFWE = 0.022, T = 4.70, d = 0.53, Z = 4.57, 100 voxels”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "observationBase": "https://iiif.elifesciences.org/epp/105391%2Fv2%2Fcontent%2F107300v6_fig4.tif/full/max,/0/default.jpg"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E34",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "The STS cluster's responses to partner prediction errors from participant and partner choices are shown for both sessions.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "That cluster's responses are shown separately for the two scanning sessions. — Anchor: “Responses to the computational-model-based regressors coding for partner reward prediction errors resulting from participant and from partner choices in this cluster are shown in Figure 4I”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "observationBase": "https://iiif.elifesciences.org/epp/105391%2Fv2%2Fcontent%2F107300v6_fig4.tif/full/max,/0/default.jpg"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E35",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Right IFG connectivity with the left insula seed was highest for risky choices for oneself and safe choices for both players (pFWE = 0.020, T = 4.34, d = 0.80, 115 voxels).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Coupling between the insula and the right inferior frontal gyrus was strongest for risky choices for oneself and safe choices for both. — Anchor: “pFWE = 0.020, T = 4.34, d = 0.80, Z = 4.21, 115 voxels, peak at MNI [46 16 22]; Figure 5”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "observationBase": "https://iiif.elifesciences.org/epp/105391%2Fv2%2Fcontent%2F107300v6_fig5.tif/full/max,/0/default.jpg"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E36",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "A smaller left IFG cluster showed the same connectivity pattern with the insula seed but did not survive correction (puncorrected = 0.001, T = 4.08, 38 voxels).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "A smaller cluster on the left showed the same pattern but did not survive statistical correction. — Anchor: “A smaller cluster in the left IFG showed the same effect but did not survive correction for multiple tests”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2",
   "observationBase": "https://iiif.elifesciences.org/epp/105391%2Fv2%2Fcontent%2F107300v6_fig5.tif/full/max,/0/default.jpg"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E37",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Left IFG connectivity with the left STS seed showed the opposite pattern — highest for safe choices for oneself and risky choices for both — uncorrected (p = 0.001, T = 4.44, 35 voxels).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Coupling between the STS and the left inferior frontal gyrus showed the opposite pattern, but did not survive correction. — Anchor: “connectivity was highest when participants made Safe choices for themselves and Risky choices for both players (puncorrected = 0.001, T = 4.44, Z = 4.30, 35 voxels”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E38",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "Dot products between individual guilt-related maps and the published guilt signature were overall positive (mean 5.22, median 6.97, sign test p = 0.017, Cliff's delta 0.4).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Participants' guilt-related brain maps resembled a published guilt signature more often than not. — Anchor: “overall these values were positive (mean = 5.22; median = 6.97; sign test: p = 0.017; Cliff’s Delta = 0.4 = medium effect size”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:E39",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Evidence",
    "Evidence"
   ],
   "title": "The signature match did not correlate with individual behavioural guilt effects (Spearman's ρ = -0.058, p = 0.725).",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "How closely a participant's map resembled the signature did not predict the size of their behavioural guilt effect. — Anchor: “did not find a significant association [Spearman’s Rho = -0.058, p = 0.725]”"
   },
   "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.105391.2"
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R1",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Ask participants directly which emotions they felt at each stage of the task, to link subjective experience to the analytical measures of guilt.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Ask participants what they felt, to connect the measures of guilt to their own experience. — Anchor: “asking participants directly about which emotions they have felt during the different stages of this task would allow us to link subjective experience with our analytical measures”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R2",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Test more participants to assess how personality traits shape the experience and the behavioural and neural correlates of guilt and responsibility.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Study more people, to see how personality shapes guilt and responsibility and their brain correlates. — Anchor: “Testing more participants would allow us to assess the impact of inter-individual variations in personality traits”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R3",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Run more trials so risk preferences can be modelled separately for gains and losses per condition, and to test whether happiness changes affect later choices.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Collect more trials, so risk preferences can be modelled separately for gains and losses in each condition. — Anchor: “Using more trials in the experiment would allow separate modelling of risk preferences in gain and loss trials in each experimental condition”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R4",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Vary the partner's identity — friend, stranger, artificial agent — to measure social discounting of guilt and responsibility.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Vary who the partner is, from friend to stranger to machine, to see how closeness changes guilt and responsibility. — Anchor: “Varying partner identities (friends, stranger, artificial agent) could reveal the impact of social discounting on guilt and responsibility”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R5",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Run the connectivity analyses on the outcome (feedback) phase of the task.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Analyse connectivity during the moment outcomes are revealed, not only during the choice. — Anchor: “Functional connectivity analyses could also be performed for the data obtained in the feedback part of the paradigm”"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "urn:claim-graphs:gadeke-2026-guilt-insula:hand:R6",
   "@type": [
    "cg:type/Request",
    "Request"
   ],
   "title": "Run experiments that isolate the specific computations the left STS/TPJ and anterior insula perform, to explain how guilt and responsibility shape social decisions.",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Design experiments that isolate what the STS and anterior insula each compute during social decisions. — Anchor: “More experiments investigating specific computations in which these regions are involved”"
   }
  },
